}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Initialisation Code",
 FontSize->36,
 FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Chapter"],
Cell["ClearAll[\"Global`*\"]", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell["(*Keep the notebook saving itself while it runs*)\nSetOptions[$FrontEndSession, NotebookAutoSave -> True]\nNotebookSave[]", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Physical Constants",
 FontSize->36]], "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{
 "The fundamental constants the simulation needs: the electron mass (",
 StyleBox["me",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "), the reduced Planck constant (",
 StyleBox["hbar",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ") and the full Planck constant (",
 StyleBox["h",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "), plus the elementary charge (",
 StyleBox["e",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ") for converting electron-volts to joules. Lengths are measured in nanometres throughout, which is why hbar carries a factor of 10^18."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Physical constants, in units built around nanometres*)\nme = 9.109*10^(-31);              (*Electron mass in kg*)\nhbar = 1.0545*10^(-34)*10^(18);   (*Reduced Planck constant in nm^2 kg s^-1*)\nh = hbar*2*Pi;                    (*Planck constant in nm^2 kg s^-1*)\ne = 1.602*10^(-19);               (*Elementary charge in C, for eV -> J*)", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[" ", "Text",
 Editable->False,
 Selectable->False,
 CellFrame->{{0, 0}, {0, 2}},
 ShowCellBracket->False,
 CellMargins->{{0, 0}, {1, 1}},
 CellElementSpacings->{"CellMinHeight"->1},
 CellFrameMargins->0,
 CellFrameColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
 CellSize->{Inherited, 4}],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["2. Simulation Framework and Finite-Difference Propagation of Quantum Wavefunctions",
 FontSize->36,
 FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Chapter"],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Simulation Set Up",
 FontSize->36]], "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{
 "The domain is a box of length ",
 StyleBox["L",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " sampled at ",
 StyleBox["numgrid",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " points; ",
 StyleBox["x",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " is the resulting grid and ",
 StyleBox["dx",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " its spacing. The finite-difference derivatives below act on this grid. Feel free to experiment: a finer grid improves accuracy at the cost of runtime, and ",
 StyleBox["framestep",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " controls how often animation frames are recorded."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Simulation Display Options:", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["(*Simulation domain parameters*)\nL = 500;              (*Length of the simulation region in nm*)\nnumgrid = 1000;       (*Number of spatial grid points*)\nntimestep = 15000;    (*Number of time steps to simulate*)\nframestep = 100;      (*Interval, in time steps, between saved animation frames*)", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Internal Set Up Calculations of Display:", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["(*Build the spatial grid. The N[] wrapper keeps everything in machine\nprecision, so Mathematica stores the arrays in packed form - the update\nsteps below run orders of magnitude faster that way than with the exact\nrationals Subdivide would otherwise produce.*)\nx = N[Subdivide[0, L, numgrid - 1]];   (*Uniform grid from 0 to L*)\ndx = x[[2]] - x[[1]];                  (*Grid spacing*)", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Physical Picture Options:", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["(*Potential parameters*)\nwellheight = 80;      (*Height/depth of the potential feature in eV*)\nwellwidth = 100;      (*Width of the potential feature in nm*)\npotential = \"step\";   (*One of \"free\", \"square\", \"quadratic\", \"step\"*)\nshift = 0;            (*Displacement of the potential in nm, relative\n                        to the centre of the simulation domain*)", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Particle Wave Options:", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["(*Wave-packet parameters*)\nwavelength = 30;      (*Wavelength in nm; sets the packet's momentum*)\ns = 20;               (*Standard deviation (width) of the Gaussian envelope*)\nwavecentre = 250;     (*Initial centre of the packet in nm*)\nwavecentreindex = Ordering[Abs[x - wavecentre], 1][[1]];\n                      (*Grid index nearest to wavecentre*)\nk = 2*Pi/wavelength;  (*Wave number*)", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Potential Well Set Up",
 FontSize->36]], "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{
 "Three static potentials are defined as piecewise functions of position: a ",
 StyleBox["square well",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", a ",
 StyleBox["quadratic (harmonic) trap",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " and a ",
 StyleBox["step barrier",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". ",
 StyleBox["choosePotential",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " samples whichever one you name onto the grid. The quadratic well is built to reach zero exactly at the edges of its region, so the parabola joins the flat potential outside continuously, and all three honour the ",
 StyleBox["shift",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " parameter."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Square well: depth -wellheight over a region of width wellwidth,\ncentred on the middle of the domain plus shift*)\nVSquare[x_] :=\n  If[x >= (L/2 - wellwidth/2 + shift) && x <= (L/2 + wellwidth/2 + shift),\n   -wellheight,\n   0];\n\n(*Quadratic (harmonic) well: a parabola that reaches -wellheight at its\nvertex and rises to exactly zero at both edges of the well region, so\nthe potential joins the flat outside continuously. The vertex follows\nshift along with the rest of the well.*)\nVQuadratic[x_] :=\n  If[Abs[x - L/2 - shift] <= wellwidth/2,\n   wellheight*((x - L/2 - shift)/(wellwidth/2))^2 - wellheight,\n   0];\n\n(*Step barrier: +wellheight over the same region*)\nVStep[x_] :=\n  If[x >= (L/2 - wellwidth/2 + shift) && x <= (L/2 + wellwidth/2 + shift),\n   wellheight,\n   0];\n\n(*Sample the requested potential on the grid.\nOptions: \"free\", \"square\", \"quadratic\", \"step\".*)\nchoosePotential[ptype_] := Module[{V},\n  V = ConstantArray[0, Length[x]];\n  Switch[ptype,\n   \"free\", Null,                  (*flat potential: keep the zeros*)\n   \"square\", V = Map[VSquare, x],\n   \"quadratic\", V = Map[VQuadratic, x],\n   \"step\", V = Map[VStep, x]];\n  V]", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell[TextData[{
 "Define a ",
 StyleBox["square potential",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " for illustration:"
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["V = choosePotential[\"square\"];", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Initial Wavefunction",
 FontSize->36]], "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{
 "The starting state is a ",
 StyleBox["Gaussian envelope",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " (localisation) multiplied by cosine and sine factors (momentum): the cosine part is the real component ",
 StyleBox["R0",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " and the sine part the imaginary component ",
 StyleBox["I0",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". Both are whole-array expressions - no loop over grid points. The state is then normalised via the ",
 StyleBox["Born rule",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " so the total probability integrates to ",
 StyleBox["unity",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", and the last line re-integrates as a check."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Gaussian envelope centred on the grid point nearest wavecentre,\nmodulated by cosine (real part) and sine (imaginary part). These are\nwhole-array operations - no loop over grid points.*)\nxc = x[[wavecentreindex]];\nenvelope = Exp[-0.5*((x - xc)/s)^2];\nR0 = envelope*Cos[k*(x - xc)];   (*Real part of the initial wavefunction*)\nI0 = envelope*Sin[k*(x - xc)];   (*Imaginary part*)\n\n(*Normalise via the Born rule: integrate |psi|^2 over the domain and\nrescale so the total probability is 1*)\nmodulus =\n  Interpolation[Transpose[{x, (R0^2 + I0^2)}], InterpolationOrder -> 1];\nA = NIntegrate[modulus[xval], {xval, 0, L}];\nR0 = R0/Sqrt[A];\nI0 = I0/Sqrt[A];\n\n(*Check the normalisation - this should return 1*)\nmodulusNormalised =\n  Interpolation[Transpose[{x, (R0^2 + I0^2)}], InterpolationOrder -> 1];\nNIntegrate[modulusNormalised[xval], {xval, 0, L}]", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Note: ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "the output above should read 1."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Visualisation of the Initial Conditions",
 FontSize->36]], "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{
 "A checkpoint before any time evolution. The first plot overlays the potential (on its own axis, in red) on the real and imaginary parts of the initial wavefunction, so you can confirm the packet sits where you intended and the potential is where you expect. ",
 StyleBox["CombinePlots",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " is fetched from the Wolfram Function Repository the first time this cell runs."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Initial wavefunction with the potential overlaid on a second axis.\nCombinePlots is fetched from the Wolfram Function Repository the first\ntime this runs, so the first evaluation needs an internet connection.*)\nResourceFunction[\"CombinePlots\"][\n ListLinePlot[{Transpose[{x, I0}], Transpose[{x, R0}]},\n  PlotRange -> All, PlotLabel -> \"Initial Wavefunction and Potential\",\n  FrameLabel -> {\"Position x [nm]\", \"Wavefunction Amplitude\"},\n  PlotStyle -> {Blue, Orange}, ImageSize -> 600,\n  Frame -> True, GridLines -> Automatic,\n  LabelStyle -> {FontSize -> 15, FontFamily -> \"Arial\", Black, Bold},\n  PlotLegends -> Placed[{\"\[Phi]I(t=0)\", \"\[Phi]R(t=0)\"}, {Left, Top}]],\n\n ListLinePlot[Transpose[{x, V}], PlotStyle -> {Red, Dotted},\n  ImageSize -> 600, BaselinePosition -> Axis,\n  PlotLegends -> Placed[{\"V(x)\"}, {Right, Top}], Frame -> True,\n  FrameStyle -> Red, PlotRange -> {Min[V] - 10, Max[V] + 10},\n  FrameLabel -> {\"Position x [nm]\", \"Potential V [eV]\"},\n  LabelStyle -> {FontSize -> 14, FontFamily -> \"Arial\", Black, Bold}],\n\n \"AxesSides\" -> \"TwoY\"]", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell[TextData[{
 "The initial probability density - the sum of squares of the two components. Its integral being one confirms ",
 StyleBox["the wavefunction is properly normalised",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Initial probability density with the potential overlaid*)\nResourceFunction[\"CombinePlots\"][\n ListLinePlot[Transpose[{x, (R0^2 + I0^2)}],\n  PlotRange -> {0, 0.07}, PlotStyle -> {Orange},\n  PlotLabel -> \"Initial Probability Distribution and Potential\",\n  FrameLabel -> {\"Position x [nm]\", \"Probability P \"},\n  ImageSize -> 600, Frame -> True, GridLines -> Automatic,\n  LabelStyle -> {FontSize -> 15, FontFamily -> \"Arial\", Black, Bold},\n  PlotLegends -> Placed[{\"P(x)\"}, {Left, Top}]],\n\n ListLinePlot[Transpose[{x, V}], PlotStyle -> {Red, Dotted},\n  ImageSize -> 600, BaselinePosition -> Axis,\n  PlotLegends -> Placed[{\"V(x)\"}, {Right, Top}], Frame -> True,\n  FrameStyle -> Red, PlotRange -> {Min[V] - 10, Max[V] + 10},\n  FrameLabel -> {\"Position x [nm]\", \"Potential V [eV]\"},\n  LabelStyle -> {FontSize -> 14, FontFamily -> \"Arial\", Black, Bold}],\n\n \"AxesSides\" -> \"TwoY\"]", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell[TextData[{
 "This confirms the ",
 StyleBox["square potential",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " is displayed as expected. The quadratic and step potentials are plotted below for comparison."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Comparison of Square, Quadratic, and Step Potentials:", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["squarePotential = choosePotential[\"square\"];\nquadraticPotential = choosePotential[\"quadratic\"];\nstepPotential = choosePotential[\"step\"];\n\nListLinePlot[{Transpose[{x, squarePotential}],\n  Transpose[{x, quadraticPotential}], Transpose[{x, stepPotential}]},\n PlotStyle -> {Dotted}, ImageSize -> Automatic,\n BaselinePosition -> Axis,\n PlotLegends ->\n  Placed[{\"Square Well\", \"Quadratic (Harmonic) Well\", \"Step Well\"},\n   {Right, Top}],\n Frame -> True, FrameStyle -> Blue,\n PlotRange -> {-wellheight - 10, wellheight + 10},\n FrameLabel -> {\"Position x [nm]\", \"Potential V [eV]\"},\n LabelStyle -> {FontSize -> 14, FontFamily -> \"Arial\", Black, Bold},\n Filling -> Bottom]", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Numerical Wave Propagation via FDTD Method",
 FontSize->36]], "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{
 "The scheme has two dimensionless constants: ",
 StyleBox["c1",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " sets how fast a free packet disperses, and ",
 StyleBox["c2",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " scales the pull of the potential. The time step ",
 StyleBox["dt",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " is derived from c1, the electron mass, the grid spacing and hbar; the factors of 10^11 and 1000 rescale the raw SI expressions so that both constants land in the numerically stable range for this grid. Times quoted by the simulation are therefore in scaled units, not literal seconds - the dynamics depend only on c1 and c2."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Finite-difference constants. c1 controls the kinetic (dispersion)\nterm and c2 scales the influence of the potential; both are\ndimensionless. The factors of 10^11 and 1000 rescale the raw SI\nexpressions so that c1 and c2 land in the numerically stable range for\nthis grid, which means times quoted by the simulation are in scaled\nunits rather than literal seconds. The dynamics depend only on c1 and\nc2, not on the absolute time scale.*)\nc1 = 1/10;                          (*Free-propagation constant*)\ndt = c1*2*me*(dx^2)*(10^11/hbar);   (*Time step, in scaled units*)\nc2 = (e*dt*1000)/hbar;              (*Potential coupling constant*)\n\nPrint[\"c1 = \", c1];\nPrint[\"dt = \", dt];\nPrint[\"c2 = \", c2];", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell[TextData[{
 "The update rule, written as whole-array operations: the ",
 StyleBox["real",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " part absorbs the second difference of the imaginary part plus the potential term, and the ",
 StyleBox["imaginary",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " part does the same with the real part and opposite signs. Span expressions such as It[[3;;]] produce the shifted arrays, so there is no inner loop over grid points - this is what makes the evolution fast enough to re-run casually."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*One finite-difference update of each component, applied to the whole\ninterior of the grid at once. Span expressions such as It[[3 ;;]] are\nthe array shifted left or right, so the bracketed combination is the\ndiscrete second derivative at every interior point simultaneously. The\nendpoints are never written to, which pins the wavefunction to zero at\nboth walls (an infinite box).*)\nupdateReal[Rt_, It_, V_] := Module[{R = Rt},\n  R[[2 ;; -2]] = R[[2 ;; -2]] -\n     c1*(It[[3 ;;]] - 2*It[[2 ;; -2]] + It[[1 ;; -3]]) +\n     c2*V[[2 ;; -2]]*It[[2 ;; -2]];\n  R];\n\nupdateImag[Rt_, It_, V_] := Module[{Inew = It},\n  Inew[[2 ;; -2]] = Inew[[2 ;; -2]] +\n     c1*(Rt[[3 ;;]] - 2*Rt[[2 ;; -2]] + Rt[[1 ;; -3]]) -\n     c2*V[[2 ;; -2]]*Rt[[2 ;; -2]];\n  Inew];", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["timeEvolutionEuler",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " wires the two updates into a time loop. The ordering matters: the real part is stepped first, then the imaginary part is stepped using the freshly updated real part. This staggered ordering (Visscher 1991) is what keeps the explicit scheme stable at c1 = 1/10. Every ",
 StyleBox["framestep",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " steps the state and its probability density are appended to the snapshot lists that the plotting section consumes."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Propagate the wavefunction. The real part is stepped first, then the\nimaginary part is stepped using the freshly updated real part; this\nstaggered ordering (Visscher 1991) is what keeps the explicit scheme\nstable. Snapshots of the state and its probability density are stored\nevery framestep steps for the animations.*)\ntimeEvolutionEuler[R0_, I0_, V_] :=\n Module[{Rt = R0, It = I0, Pt, times = {}, RtFrames = {}, ItFrames = {},\n   PtFrames = {}},\n  Do[\n   Rt = updateReal[Rt, It, V];\n   It = updateImag[Rt, It, V];\n   If[Mod[nt, framestep] == 0,\n    AppendTo[RtFrames, Rt];\n    AppendTo[ItFrames, It];\n    AppendTo[times, nt*dt];\n    Pt = Rt^2 + It^2;\n    AppendTo[PtFrames, Pt]],\n   {nt, 1, ntimestep}];\n  {times, RtFrames, ItFrames, PtFrames}]", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["As a Side Note: Using a Runge\[Dash]Kutta Scheme",
 FontSize->36]], "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{
 "The staggered scheme above is what the final simulations use. This section implements a fourth-order ",
 StyleBox["Runge-Kutta (RK4)",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " integrator as an alternative: four intermediate slopes ",
 StyleBox["(k1, k2, k3, k4)",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " per step, combined for a global error of ",
 StyleBox["O(\[CapitalDelta]t^4)",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". A few traps are worth knowing about when writing an RK4 for this scheme: the derivative signs have to match the Euler update, dt must not be applied twice (it already lives inside c1 and c2), and all four snapshot lists have to be appended inside the same If or they come out different lengths. The implementation below treats the k's as per-step increments, combined in the standard increment form, and its snapshot bookkeeping matches timeEvolutionEuler exactly, so the two integrators are interchangeable."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Optional:",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " RK4 also opens the door to ",
 StyleBox["time-dependent",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " potentials, since each stage can re-evaluate V at its own instant. Two examples are wired in: a switching potential that jumps from V1 to V2 at time t0, and an oscillatory one modulated by Cos[\[Omega]t]; ",
 StyleBox["UnitBox",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " confines both to a finite region. ",
 StyleBox["ComputeDerivatives",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " takes the time t so it can sample them dynamically; its second difference uses ",
 StyleBox["RotateLeft",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["RotateRight",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " (periodic wrap-around), softened by the absorbing sponge defined further down."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*---Extended potential library: time-dependent potentials---*)\n\n(*Switching potential: V1 before time t0, V2 after. UnitBox confines\nthe potential to a region of width wellwidth around the centre.*)\ntimeDependentPotential[x_, t_] :=\n  If[t < t0, V1, V2]*UnitBox[(x - L/2)/wellwidth];\n\n(*Oscillatory potential with cosine time modulation*)\noscillatoryPotential[x_, t_] :=\n  V0*Cos[\[Omega]*t]*UnitBox[(x - L/2)/wellwidth];\n\n(*Parameters for the time-dependent potentials. The switching time and\nthe oscillation frequency are set relative to the total simulated time,\nso the switch actually happens (and the oscillation completes two full\nperiods) within a run.*)\nt0 = 0.5*ntimestep*dt;            (*Switch halfway through the run*)\nV1 = -80;                         (*Potential before the switch, in eV*)\nV2 = 80;                          (*Potential after the switch*)\nV0 = 50;                          (*Amplitude of the oscillatory potential*)\n\[Omega] = 4*Pi/(ntimestep*dt);         (*Two oscillation periods per run*)\n\n(*Per-step increment of each component. The signs match the Euler\nupdate above: dR carries -c1 times the second difference of the\nimaginary part, dI carries +c1 times that of the real part. Because c1\nand c2 already contain dt, these are increments over one step rather\nthan raw time derivatives - RK4Step combines them accordingly. The\npotential is re-evaluated at time t when it is time-dependent, and the\nsecond difference is built with RotateLeft/RotateRight (periodic\nwrap-around), which the absorbing sponge below keeps in check.*)\nComputeDerivatives[Rt_, It_, t_, V_] :=\n Module[{Vt, dR, dI},\n  Vt = If[potential === \"time-dependent\",\n    Table[timeDependentPotential[x[[i]], t], {i, Length[x]}],\n    V];\n  dR = -c1*(RotateLeft[It] - 2*It + RotateRight[It]) + c2*Vt*It;\n  dI = c1*(RotateLeft[Rt] - 2*Rt + RotateRight[Rt]) - c2*Vt*Rt;\n  {dR, dI}]", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 1]],
Cell[TextData[{
 "Below is an example of how the ",
 StyleBox["RK4 method",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " can be implemented:"
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*One fourth-order Runge-Kutta step. Each k is a per-step increment (dt\nalready lives inside c1 and c2), so the stages use k/2 and k rather\nthan dt*k/2 - this is standard RK4 written in increment form. The\nhalf-step stages are evaluated at t + dt/2 so that a time-dependent\npotential is sampled at the right instants.*)\nRK4Step[Rt_, It_, t_, V_] :=\n Module[{k1, k2, k3, k4},\n  k1 = ComputeDerivatives[Rt, It, t, V];\n  k2 = ComputeDerivatives[Rt + k1[[1]]/2, It + k1[[2]]/2, t + dt/2, V];\n  k3 = ComputeDerivatives[Rt + k2[[1]]/2, It + k2[[2]]/2, t + dt/2, V];\n  k4 = ComputeDerivatives[Rt + k3[[1]], It + k3[[2]], t + dt, V];\n  {Rt + (k1[[1]] + 2*k2[[1]] + 2*k3[[1]] + k4[[1]])/6,\n   It + (k1[[2]] + 2*k2[[2]] + 2*k3[[2]] + k4[[2]])/6}]", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 1]],
Cell["(*Absorbing sponge: damps the outer 10% of the grid on each side so\noutgoing waves die away instead of reflecting (or wrapping around,\ngiven the cyclic second difference above). Interior points are left\nuntouched.*)\nAbsorbingBoundary[psi_] := Module[{n = Length[psi], d, absorber},\n  d = Floor[0.1*n];\n  absorber = Table[Which[\n     i <= d, Exp[-((d - i)/d)^2],\n     i > n - d, Exp[-((i - (n - d))/d)^2],\n     True, 1],\n    {i, 1, n}];\n  psi*absorber]", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 1]],
Cell["(*RK4 time evolution with the same frame-recording interface as\ntimeEvolutionEuler, so the two are interchangeable everywhere below.\nAll four snapshot lists are appended inside the same If, which keeps\nthem the same length.*)\ntimeEvolutionRK4[R0_, I0_, V_] :=\n Module[{Rt = R0, It = I0, Pt, currentTime = 0., times = {},\n   RtFrames = {}, ItFrames = {}, PtFrames = {}},\n  Do[\n   {Rt, It} = RK4Step[Rt, It, currentTime, V];\n   currentTime += dt;\n   Rt = AbsorbingBoundary[Rt];\n   It = AbsorbingBoundary[It];\n   If[Mod[nt, framestep] == 0,\n    AppendTo[RtFrames, Rt];\n    AppendTo[ItFrames, It];\n    AppendTo[times, currentTime];\n    Pt = Rt^2 + It^2;\n    AppendTo[PtFrames, Pt]],\n   {nt, 1, ntimestep}];\n  {times, RtFrames, ItFrames, PtFrames}]", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 1]],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Note: ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "RK4 buys accuracy per step but costs four derivative evaluations each. For this project the staggered scheme is accurate enough, so the Chapter 3 runs default to it; the RK4 cells there are optional drop-in replacements."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Creation of the Time Evolution Plots",
 FontSize->36]], "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["generateEvolutionPlots",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " turns the snapshots from either evolution function into one composite figure per frame: the wavefunction components with the potential overlaid, above the probability density with the same overlay, stamped with the elapsed (scaled) time. ",
 StyleBox["ListAnimate",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " then plays the list back as a smooth animation."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Build one composite figure per saved frame: wavefunction components\nwith the potential overlaid on a second axis, above the probability\ndensity with the same overlay, stamped with the elapsed (scaled) time.\nevolutionPlots is local, so the function's only effect is its return\nvalue.*)\ngenerateEvolutionPlots[result_, V_] :=\n Module[{times, RtFrames, ItFrames, PtFrames, evolutionPlots},\n  {times, RtFrames, ItFrames, PtFrames} = result;\n\n  evolutionPlots = Table[\n    GraphicsColumn[{\n\n      ResourceFunction[\"CombinePlots\"][\n       ListLinePlot[{Transpose[{x, ItFrames[[frameNo]]}],\n         Transpose[{x, RtFrames[[frameNo]]}]},\n        PlotRange -> {{0, L}, {-0.25, 0.25}},\n        PlotLabel -> \"Wavefunction and Potential\",\n        FrameLabel -> {\"Position x [nm]\", \"Wavefunction Amplitude\"},\n        PlotStyle -> {Blue, Orange}, ImageSize -> Automatic,\n        Frame -> True, GridLines -> Automatic,\n        LabelStyle -> {FontSize -> 14, FontFamily -> \"Arial\", Black, Bold},\n        PlotLegends -> Placed[{\"\[Phi]I(t)\", \"\[Phi]R(t)\"}, {Left, Top}]],\n       ListLinePlot[Transpose[{x, V}], PlotStyle -> {Red, Dotted},\n        ImageSize -> Automatic, BaselinePosition -> Axis,\n        PlotLegends -> Placed[{\"V(x)\"}, {Right, Top}], Frame -> True,\n        FrameStyle -> Red, PlotRange -> {Min[V] - 10, Max[V] + 10},\n        FrameLabel -> {\"Position x [nm]\", \"Potential V [eV]\"},\n        LabelStyle -> {FontSize -> 14, FontFamily -> \"Arial\", Black, Bold}],\n       \"AxesSides\" -> \"TwoY\"],\n\n      ResourceFunction[\"CombinePlots\"][\n       ListLinePlot[Transpose[{x, PtFrames[[frameNo]]}],\n        PlotRange -> {{0, L}, {0, Max[PtFrames] + 0.01}}, PlotStyle -> Red,\n        PlotLabel -> \"Probability Distribution and Potential\",\n        FrameLabel -> {\"Position x [nm]\", \"Probability P \"},\n        ImageSize -> Automatic, Frame -> True, GridLines -> Automatic,\n        LabelStyle -> {FontSize -> 15, FontFamily -> \"Arial\", Black, Bold},\n        PlotLegends -> Placed[{\"P(x)\"}, {Left, Top}]],\n       ListLinePlot[Transpose[{x, V}], PlotStyle -> {Red, Dotted},\n        ImageSize -> Automatic, BaselinePosition -> Axis,\n        PlotLegends -> Placed[{\"V(x)\"}, {Right, Top}], Frame -> True,\n        FrameStyle -> Red, PlotRange -> {Min[V] - 10, Max[V] + 10},\n        FrameLabel -> {\"Position x [nm]\", \"Potential V [eV]\"},\n        LabelStyle -> {FontSize -> 14, FontFamily -> \"Arial\", Black, Bold}],\n       \"AxesSides\" -> \"TwoY\"]\n      },\n     ImageSize -> Large,\n     Epilog -> Inset[\n       Framed[Text[Style[\n          \"Elapsed time = \" <>\n           ToString[NumberForm[times[[frameNo]], {2, 2}]] <>\n           \" (scaled units)\",\n          FontFamily -> \"Arial\", FontSize -> 12, FontColor -> Black,\n          Bold]]],\n       Scaled[{0.75, 0.08}]]],\n    {frameNo, 1, Length[RtFrames], 1}];\n\n  evolutionPlots]", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[" ", "Text",
 Editable->False,
 Selectable->False,
 CellFrame->{{0, 0}, {0, 2}},
 ShowCellBracket->False,
 CellMargins->{{0, 0}, {1, 1}},
 CellElementSpacings->{"CellMinHeight"->1},
 CellFrameMargins->0,
 CellFrameColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
 CellSize->{Inherited, 4}],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["3. Quantum Dynamics in Action: Simulating Time Evolution Across Diverse Potential Landscapes",
 FontSize->36,
 FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Chapter"],
Cell[TextData[{
 "This chapter runs the solver across ",
 StyleBox["four potential landscapes",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", starting from the free particle. Each section follows the same pattern: fetch a potential, evolve the packet, build the plots, animate, and optionally export the animation to the videos folder."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Free Particle",
 FontSize->36]], "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{
 "The simplest case first: no potential at all (",
 StyleBox["V(x) = 0",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "), so the packet evolves under its kinetic term alone. A useful baseline - it validates the propagation machinery and shows dispersion with nothing else in the way."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Flat potential for the free-particle run*)\nfreePotential = choosePotential[\"free\"];", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell["(*Evolve the packet with the staggered explicit scheme*)\nfreeResult = timeEvolutionEuler[R0, I0, freePotential];", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Optional: ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "the same run through ",
 StyleBox["timeEvolutionRK4",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". Slower (four derivative evaluations per step) and visually identical here, but after the Chapter 2 fixes it works."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Optional: the same evolution with the RK4 integrator*)\nfreeResult = timeEvolutionRK4[R0, I0, freePotential];", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 1]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Generate the Plots and the Resultant Animation", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["(*One composite plot per saved frame*)\nfreePlots = generateEvolutionPlots[freeResult, freePotential];", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell["(*Play the frames back as an animation*)\nevolAnimFree = ListAnimate[freePlots,\n  DefaultDuration -> 7, AnimationRepetitions -> 1,\n  DisplayAllSteps -> True]", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell["(*Export the animation next to the notebook*)\nIf[! DirectoryQ[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\"}]],\n  CreateDirectory[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\"}]]];\n\nExport[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\", \"FreeParticle.mp4\"}],\n freePlots, \"AnimationDuration\" -> 10]\n\nExport[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\", \"FreeParticle.gif\"}],\n freePlots, \"AnimationDuration\" -> 10]", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 1]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Physical Interpretation:",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "\nThe packet drifts at the speed set by its ",
 StyleBox["wavevector",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " and spreads as it travels: a free quantum particle does not stay localised. By the end of the run the envelope is visibly broader than at the start, with no external force involved."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Finite Square Potential Well",
 FontSize->36]], "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{
 "A ",
 StyleBox["finite square well",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " traps the particle; the packet's energy sits at roughly half the well depth."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Square-well configuration: centred, 100 nm wide*)\nshift = 0;\nwellwidth = 100;\n\nsquarePotential = choosePotential[\"square\"];", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell["(*Evolve the packet with the staggered explicit scheme*)\nsquareResult = timeEvolutionEuler[R0, I0, squarePotential];", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Optional: ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "swap in the RK4 integrator below - it is interchangeable with the Euler cell above."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Optional: the same evolution with the RK4 integrator*)\nsquareResult = timeEvolutionRK4[R0, I0, squarePotential];", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 1]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Generate the Plots and the Resultant Animation", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["(*One composite plot per saved frame*)\nsquarePlots = generateEvolutionPlots[squareResult, squarePotential];", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell["(*Play the frames back as an animation*)\nevolAnimSquare = ListAnimate[squarePlots,\n  DefaultDuration -> 7, AnimationRepetitions -> 1,\n  DisplayAllSteps -> True]", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell["(*Export the animation next to the notebook*)\nIf[! DirectoryQ[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\"}]],\n  CreateDirectory[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\"}]]];\n\nExport[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\", \"FiniteSquareWell.mp4\"}],\n squarePlots, \"AnimationDuration\" -> 10]\n\nExport[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\", \"FiniteSquareWell.gif\"}],\n squarePlots, \"AnimationDuration\" -> 10]", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 1]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Physical Interpretation:",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "\nThe packet bounces back and forth between the walls of the well. At each reflection a little probability leaks into the classically forbidden region and decays there - ",
 StyleBox["tunnelling",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " - while reflected and incoming components interfere, producing the ripples in the density."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Quadratic (Harmonic) Potential Well",
 FontSize->36]], "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{
 "A packet in an idealised ",
 StyleBox["harmonic",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " trap, one of the standard model systems in quantum mechanics. The well width is doubled to 200 nm to give the packet room to oscillate."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Harmonic-well configuration: centred, widened to\n200 nm so the packet has room to oscillate*)\nshift = 0;\nwellwidth = 200;\n\npotentialQuadratic = choosePotential[\"quadratic\"];", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell["(*Evolve the packet with the staggered explicit scheme*)\nquadraticResult = timeEvolutionEuler[R0, I0, potentialQuadratic];", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Optional: ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "the RK4 version of the same run."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Optional: the same evolution with the RK4 integrator*)\nquadraticResult = timeEvolutionRK4[R0, I0, potentialQuadratic];", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 1]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Generate the Plots and the Resultant Animation", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["(*One composite plot per saved frame*)\nquadraticPlots = generateEvolutionPlots[quadraticResult, potentialQuadratic];", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell["(*Play the frames back as an animation*)\nevolAnimQuadratic = ListAnimate[quadraticPlots,\n  DefaultDuration -> 7, AnimationRepetitions -> 1,\n  DisplayAllSteps -> True]", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell["(*Export the animation next to the notebook*)\nIf[! DirectoryQ[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\"}]],\n  CreateDirectory[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\"}]]];\n\nExport[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\", \"QuadraticWell.mp4\"}],\n quadraticPlots, \"AnimationDuration\" -> 10]\n\nExport[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\", \"QuadraticWell.gif\"}],\n quadraticPlots, \"AnimationDuration\" -> 10]", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 1]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Physical Interpretation:",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "\nThe packet sloshes about the minimum of the parabola and refocuses each time it turns. The pronounced ",
 StyleBox["\"wiggling\"",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " of the amplitudes is interference between components running through each other. There is no hard wall here, so the turning points are soft: the density climbs a little way up the potential before falling back."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Step Potential Well",
 FontSize->36]], "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{
 "A travelling packet meets a ",
 StyleBox["step potential barrier",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". The step is ",
 StyleBox["shifted 150 nm to the right",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", directly into the path of the packet (which starts at the centre moving right), so reflection, transmission and quantum tunnelling all show up mid-run."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Step-barrier configuration: shifted 150 nm to the\nright, directly into the path of the packet*)\nshift = 150;\nwellwidth = 100;\n\npotentialStep = choosePotential[\"step\"];", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell["(*Evolve the packet with the staggered explicit scheme*)\nstepResult = timeEvolutionEuler[R0, I0, potentialStep];", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Optional: ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "the RK4 route, if you want to compare integrators here."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Optional: the same evolution with the RK4 integrator*)\nstepResult = timeEvolutionRK4[R0, I0, potentialStep];", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 1]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Generate the Plots and the Resultant Animation", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["(*One composite plot per saved frame*)\nstepPlots = generateEvolutionPlots[stepResult, potentialStep];", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell["(*Play the frames back as an animation*)\nevolAnimStep = ListAnimate[stepPlots,\n  DefaultDuration -> 7, AnimationRepetitions -> 1,\n  DisplayAllSteps -> True]", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell["(*Export the animation next to the notebook*)\nIf[! DirectoryQ[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\"}]],\n  CreateDirectory[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\"}]]];\n\nExport[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\", \"StepWell.mp4\"}],\n stepPlots, \"AnimationDuration\" -> 10]\n\nExport[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\", \"StepWell.gif\"}],\n stepPlots, \"AnimationDuration\" -> 10]", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 1]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Physical Interpretation:",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "\nMost of the packet reflects. During the collision the incident and reflected parts pile up in front of the barrier and interfere, briefly compressing the probability density. Inside the barrier the density falls off ",
 StyleBox["exponentially",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " with depth - the signature of ",
 StyleBox["tunnelling",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Customise Simulation Parameters: Potential and Wave Packet Settings",
 FontSize->36]], "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["The orange cells below collect the knobs worth turning: the potential (\"Physical Picture Options\") and the initial packet (\"Particle Wave Options\"). Change them, re-evaluate the cells that follow, and watch what the dynamics do.",
  FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Physical Picture Options", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["(*Potential parameters*)\nwellheight = 80;      (*Height/depth of the potential feature in eV*)\nwellwidth = 100;      (*Width of the potential feature in nm*)\npotential = \"step\";   (*One of \"free\", \"square\", \"quadratic\", \"step\"*)\nshift = 0;            (*Displacement of the potential in nm, relative\n                        to the centre of the simulation domain*)", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 0.8]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Particle Wave Options", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["(*Wave-packet parameters*)\nwavelength = 30;      (*Wavelength in nm; sets the packet's momentum*)\ns = 20;               (*Standard deviation (width) of the Gaussian envelope*)\nwavecentre = 250;     (*Initial centre of the packet in nm*)\nwavecentreindex = Ordering[Abs[x - wavecentre], 1][[1]];\n                      (*Grid index nearest to wavecentre*)\nk = 2*Pi/wavelength;  (*Wave number*)", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 0.8]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Recalculate the Initial Wavefunction", "Subsubsection"],
Cell[TextData[{
 "Rebuild ",
 StyleBox["R0",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["I0",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " from the current parameter values and re-normalise - the same construction as Chapter 2."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Gaussian envelope centred on the grid point nearest wavecentre,\nmodulated by cosine (real part) and sine (imaginary part). These are\nwhole-array operations - no loop over grid points.*)\nxc = x[[wavecentreindex]];\nenvelope = Exp[-0.5*((x - xc)/s)^2];\nR0 = envelope*Cos[k*(x - xc)];   (*Real part of the initial wavefunction*)\nI0 = envelope*Sin[k*(x - xc)];   (*Imaginary part*)\n\n(*Normalise via the Born rule: integrate |psi|^2 over the domain and\nrescale so the total probability is 1*)\nmodulus =\n  Interpolation[Transpose[{x, (R0^2 + I0^2)}], InterpolationOrder -> 1];\nA = NIntegrate[modulus[xval], {xval, 0, L}];\nR0 = R0/Sqrt[A];\nI0 = I0/Sqrt[A];\n\n(*Check the normalisation - this should return 1*)\nmodulusNormalised =\n  Interpolation[Transpose[{x, (R0^2 + I0^2)}], InterpolationOrder -> 1];\nNIntegrate[modulusNormalised[xval], {xval, 0, L}]", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Retrieve the Desired Potential", "Subsubsection"],
Cell[TextData[{
 "Evolve the packet in the potential selected above (\"free\", \"square\", \"quadratic\" or \"step\"), using the ",
 StyleBox["Euler",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " scheme."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Sample the potential named by the \"potential\" parameter above*)\npotentialUser = choosePotential[potential];", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 0.8]],
Cell["(*Evolve with the staggered explicit scheme*)\nuserResult = timeEvolutionEuler[R0, I0, potentialUser];", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 0.8]],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Optional: ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "or with RK4 instead:"
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Or evolve with the RK4 integrator instead*)\nuserResult = timeEvolutionRK4[R0, I0, potentialUser];", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 1]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Generate the Plots and the Resultant Animation", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["(*One composite plot per saved frame*)\nuserPlots = generateEvolutionPlots[userResult, potentialUser];", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 0.8]],
Cell["(*Play the frames back as an animation*)\nevolAnimUser = ListAnimate[userPlots,\n  DefaultDuration -> 7, AnimationRepetitions -> 1,\n  DisplayAllSteps -> True]", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 0.8]],
Cell["(*Export the animation next to the notebook*)\nIf[! DirectoryQ[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\"}]],\n  CreateDirectory[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\"}]]];\n\nExport[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\", \"UserParticleWell.mp4\"}],\n userPlots, \"AnimationDuration\" -> 10]\n\nExport[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"videos\", \"UserParticleWell.gif\"}],\n userPlots, \"AnimationDuration\" -> 10]", "Input",
 CellDingbat->"\[WatchIcon]",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 1]]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Interactive Simulation Dashboard",
 FontSize->36]], "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{
 "A self-contained ",
 StyleBox["Manipulate",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " for quick experiments. Sliders set the potential type and geometry, the packet width and wavelength, and how many steps to run; every change re-runs the simulation from scratch and redraws two panels - the final probability density and the expectation value ",
 StyleBox["\[LeftAngleBracket]x\[RightAngleBracket](t)",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " over the run. Updates fire when you release a slider, not continuously; each re-run takes a second or two."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Self-contained interactive dashboard. Each control change rebuilds\nthe packet and the potential from the slider values, re-runs a\nsimulation of nsteps steps, and redraws two panels: the final\nprobability density (with the potential on a second axis) and the\nexpectation value <x>(t) over the run. ContinuousAction -> False defers\nthe update until a slider is released; each re-run takes a second or\ntwo.*)\nManipulate[\n Module[{kD, xcD, envD, RD, ID, AD, VD, PD, xExp = {}, tExp = {}},\n  kD = 2*Pi/lam;\n  xcD = 250.;\n  envD = Exp[-0.5*((x - xcD)/sw)^2];\n  RD = envD*Cos[kD*(x - xcD)];\n  ID = envD*Sin[kD*(x - xcD)];\n  AD = dx*Total[RD^2 + ID^2];\n  RD = RD/Sqrt[AD];\n  ID = ID/Sqrt[AD];\n  VD = Which[\n    ptype === \"free\", 0.*x,\n    ptype === \"square\",\n     Map[If[Abs[# - L/2 - sh] <= ww/2, -wh, 0.] &, x],\n    ptype === \"quadratic\",\n     Map[If[Abs[# - L/2 - sh] <= ww/2,\n        wh*((# - L/2 - sh)/(ww/2))^2 - wh, 0.] &, x],\n    ptype === \"step\",\n     Map[If[Abs[# - L/2 - sh] <= ww/2, 1.*wh, 0.] &, x]];\n  Do[\n   RD[[2 ;; -2]] = RD[[2 ;; -2]] -\n      c1*(ID[[3 ;;]] - 2*ID[[2 ;; -2]] + ID[[1 ;; -3]]) +\n      c2*VD[[2 ;; -2]]*ID[[2 ;; -2]];\n   ID[[2 ;; -2]] = ID[[2 ;; -2]] +\n      c1*(RD[[3 ;;]] - 2*RD[[2 ;; -2]] + RD[[1 ;; -3]]) -\n      c2*VD[[2 ;; -2]]*RD[[2 ;; -2]];\n   If[Mod[nt, framestep] == 0,\n    PD = RD^2 + ID^2;\n    AppendTo[xExp, dx*Total[x*PD]];\n    AppendTo[tExp, nt*dt]],\n   {nt, 1, nsteps}];\n  PD = RD^2 + ID^2;\n  Column[{\n    ResourceFunction[\"CombinePlots\"][\n     ListLinePlot[Transpose[{x, PD}], PlotRange -> All,\n      PlotStyle -> Orange, Filling -> Bottom, Frame -> True,\n      FrameLabel -> {\"Position x [nm]\", \"Probability P\"},\n      PlotLabel -> \"Final probability density\", ImageSize -> 480],\n     ListLinePlot[Transpose[{x, VD}], PlotStyle -> {Red, Dotted},\n      BaselinePosition -> Axis, Frame -> True, FrameStyle -> Red,\n      PlotRange -> {Min[VD] - 10, Max[VD] + 10}, ImageSize -> 480],\n     \"AxesSides\" -> \"TwoY\"],\n    ListLinePlot[Transpose[{tExp, xExp}], Frame -> True,\n     FrameLabel -> {\"Time t [scaled units]\",\n       \"\[LeftAngleBracket]x\[RightAngleBracket] [nm]\"},\n     PlotLabel -> \"Expectation value \[LeftAngleBracket]x\[RightAngleBracket](t)\",\n     ImageSize -> 480, PlotStyle -> Blue]\n    }]],\n {{ptype, \"square\", \"potential\"}, {\"free\", \"square\", \"quadratic\", \"step\"}},\n {{wh, 80, \"well height [eV]\"}, 10, 200, 10},\n {{ww, 100, \"well width [nm]\"}, 40, 300, 10},\n {{sh, 0, \"shift [nm]\"}, -150, 150, 10},\n {{sw, 20, \"packet width s [nm]\"}, 5, 50, 5},\n {{lam, 30, \"wavelength [nm]\"}, 10, 80, 5},\n {{nsteps, 6000, \"time steps\"}, 1000, 15000, 1000},\n ControlPlacement -> Left,\n ContinuousAction -> False,\n SynchronousUpdating -> False,\n TrackedSymbols :> {ptype, wh, ww, sh, sw, lam, nsteps}]", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[1, 0.9, 0.8]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Advanced Visualisations \[Dash] Phase-Space and Frequency Analysis",
 FontSize->36]], "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{
 "Two further ways of looking at a finished run. The first cell builds a discrete ",
 StyleBox["Wigner function",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " - a quasi-probability map over position and momentum - for any saved frame, behind a frame slider. Structure that is invisible in position space, such as the opposite-momentum components of a reflected packet, separates cleanly here. The second cell scans a ",
 StyleBox["Periodogram",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " window across the final state, so you can compare the local frequency content on either side of a barrier. Both cells read their frames from ",
 StyleBox["stepResult",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "; point them at any other Chapter 3 result to analyse a different run."
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["(*Phase-space view of a finished run. WignerFunction builds a discrete\nWigner transform: at each position the wavefunction is correlated with\nits mirror image and the correlation is Fourier-transformed, giving a\nquasi-probability map over position and momentum. Out-of-range points\ncount as zero (no wrap-around), which avoids the ghost image at half\nthe grid that a cyclic correlation would produce. Frames are unpacked\nfrom stepResult - point this at freeResult, squareResult,\nquadraticResult or userResult to inspect a different run.*)\n{wTimes, wRtFrames, wItFrames, wPtFrames} = stepResult;\n\nWignerFunction[psi_] :=\n Module[{n = Length[psi], corr},\n  Table[\n   corr = Table[\n     If[1 <= i + j <= n && 1 <= i - j <= n,\n      psi[[i + j]]*Conjugate[psi[[i - j]]],\n      0.],\n     {j, 0, n - 1}];\n   Re[Fourier[corr]],\n   {i, 1, n}]];\n\n(*Subsampling the 1000-point grid by 4 keeps each map under a second*)\nManipulate[\n ListDensityPlot[\n  Transpose[WignerFunction[\n    (wRtFrames[[frameIndex]] + I*wItFrames[[frameIndex]])[[1 ;; -1 ;; 4]]]],\n  PlotLabel -> \"Phase-Space Dynamics\",\n  FrameLabel -> {\"Position (subsampled grid index)\",\n    \"Momentum (Fourier index)\"},\n  PlotRange -> All, ColorFunction -> \"SunsetColors\",\n  ImageSize -> 480],\n {{frameIndex, 1, \"frame\"}, 1, Length[wRtFrames], 1},\n ContinuousAction -> False, SynchronousUpdating -> False]", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]],
Cell["(*Local frequency content of the final state: a Periodogram of the\nprobability density in a window of +/-50 grid points around a movable\nprobe. Slide the probe across the barrier to compare the spectrum on\neither side.*)\nManipulate[\n Periodogram[\n  wRtFrames[[-1]][[probeIndex - 50 ;; probeIndex + 50]]^2 +\n   wItFrames[[-1]][[probeIndex - 50 ;; probeIndex + 50]]^2,\n  PlotLabel -> \"Frequency Spectrum at the Probe\",\n  PlotRange -> All, Frame -> True, ImageSize -> 480],\n {{probeIndex, Round[numgrid/2], \"probe position (grid index)\"},\n  51, numgrid - 50, 1},\n ContinuousAction -> False]", "Input",
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[0.88, 1, 0.88]]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],
Cell[" ", "Text",
 Editable->False,
 Selectable->False,
 CellFrame->{{0, 0}, {0, 2}},
 ShowCellBracket->False,
 CellMargins->{{0, 0}, {1, 1}},
 CellElementSpacings->{"CellMinHeight"->1},
 CellFrameMargins->0,
 CellFrameColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
 CellSize->{Inherited, 4}],
Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["4. Conclusion",
 FontSize->36,
 FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Chapter"],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Robust Numerical Framework:",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["\t\[Bullet]\tThe TDSE is solved with an explicit staggered finite-difference scheme, vectorised over the grid. It is simple, stable at the chosen c1, and fast enough that a full 15000-step run takes seconds.", "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Solid Foundational Setup:",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["\t\[Bullet]\tConstants, grid and units are fixed in one place. Times are quoted in scaled units - Chapter 2 spells out exactly where the scaling enters.", "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Flexible Potential Landscape:",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["\t\[Bullet]\tOne function, choosePotential, switches between free, square, quadratic and step landscapes; the RK4 branch extends this to time-dependent potentials.", "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Accurate Time Propagation:",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["\t\[Bullet]\tThe initial state is a normalised Gaussian packet whose momentum is encoded through its cosine and sine factors; a corrected RK4 integrator is available as a higher-order alternative to the default scheme.", "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Interactive Exploration:",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["\t\[Bullet]\tThe dashboard re-runs the simulation as you move sliders and tracks the expectation value of position, which makes parameter dependence quick to explore.", "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Insightful Visualisations:",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["\t\[Bullet]\tEvery run becomes an animation of the wavefunction and its probability density; the Wigner map and the windowed Periodogram add phase-space and frequency views on top.", "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Future Extensions:",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["\t\[Bullet]\tTwo spatial dimensions, and driving the time-dependent potentials properly (they are wired in but only demonstrated in passing), are the obvious next steps.", "Text",
 FontSize->16],
Cell["All in all: discretise, split the wavefunction into real and imaginary parts, step forward, and watch. The abstract machinery of the TDSE turns into something you can actually see.", "Text",
 FontSize->16]
}, Open  ]],
